  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule, and the rule is enabled. Its first run succeeded with no alerts. I didn't open the dashboard in the browser to check that its panels render, and I didn't run a separate search to confirm the rule's 5xx filter actually matches documents.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` has one document per web request, with `timestamp` as its time field. The main fields are:\n- `response`: the HTTP status code, stored as text. The keyword version, `response.keyword`, is the one you can filter and group on.\n- `bytes`: the response size.\n- `clientip`: the visitor's IP address.\n- `url`, `request`, `extension`, `referer`, `agent` and `machine.os`: what was requested and by which client.\n- `geo.src`, `geo.dest` and `geo.coordinates`: where the request came from and went to.\n- `hour_of_day`: an extra field calculated from the timestamp.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-322d14fa Web logs overview\"** (id `78fa28e9-420c-47b2-9a08-f6c438e6ee13`, default time range is the last 7 days) has 8 panels:\n- Four single-number tiles: total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`), average response size in bytes, and 5xx error count.\n- A stacked bar chart of requests over time, split by response code.\n- A donut chart of requests by response code.\n- A table of the top 10 URLs, with request count and total bytes.\n- A bar chart of the top 10 source countries.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-322d14fa 5xx spike\"** (id `d6f2a3be-1c3a-4744-acab-5dc8ded79c58`) is an Elasticsearch query rule:\n- It runs every minute.\n- It counts requests matching `response.keyword : 5*` in the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is more than 10.\n- I turned off the \"exclude hits from previous run\" option. Each check then counts every 5xx in the full 5-minute window, even if an earlier check already counted it.\n- It has no notification actions, so a firing alert will only show in Kibana. Add a connector if you want it to send emails or messages.",
